window.OS_CARDS = [{"n":0.1,"ti":"C for Systems: Pointers & Memory","f":"p1-c-for-systems.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"After int x = 5; int *p = &x; *p = 7; , what is the value of x ?","o":["5, because p is a copy","7, because writing through p changes the int at x's address","The address of x","It doesn't compile"],"a":1,"w":"p holds the address of x , so *p = 7 writes 7 into x 's four bytes."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"C for Systems: Pointers & Memory","f":"p1-c-for-systems.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why does read(fd, buf, n) take a pointer to a buffer instead of returning the data?","o":["Pointers are faster than return values","The kernel cannot return integers","C passes arguments by value, so to fill memory the caller owns, the kernel needs that memory's address","So that read() can call free() on it"],"a":2,"w":"The caller owns the buffer and passes its address; the kernel copies file data into it and returns only the byte count (or −1 with errno )."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"C for Systems: Pointers & Memory","f":"p1-c-for-systems.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A function declares char buf[64]; , fills it and returns buf . What goes wrong?","o":["buf lives in the function's stack frame, which is gone after return, so the caller gets a dangling pointer","Nothing: arrays are always on the heap","It leaks 64 bytes","It is a double free"],"a":0,"w":"Locals die with their frame. The next function call reuses those bytes, so the caller's \"string\" changes under it. Return heap memory (and document who frees it) or let the caller pass a buffer in."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"C for Systems: Pointers & Memory","f":"p1-c-for-systems.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is sizeof p when char *p = malloc(1000); on x86-64 Linux?","o":["1000","1","4","8: it is the size of the pointer itself, not the block it points to"],"a":3,"w":"A pointer is an 8-byte address on a 64-bit machine. C does not remember the size of the block; you must carry the length yourself."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"C for Systems: Pointers & Memory","f":"p1-c-for-systems.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A program frees a block and later writes to it through the old pointer. Why is this so dangerous?","o":["It always crashes immediately","The allocator may have handed the block to other code, so the write silently corrupts unrelated data","The kernel deletes the process's heap","It only leaks memory"],"a":1,"w":"Use-after-free rarely crashes at once; it corrupts whatever now lives in the block, often far from the bug, and attackers can arrange for useful objects to land there."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Structs, Arrays & Strings in C","f":"p2-structs-arrays-strings.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"On x86-64, what is sizeof(struct { char a; double d; char b; }) ?","o":["10","16","24","17"],"a":2,"w":"a at 0, 7 bytes padding, d at 8–15, b at 16, then 7 bytes of tail padding to a multiple of 8: 24. Putting d first gives 16."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Structs, Arrays & Strings in C","f":"p2-structs-arrays-strings.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"With uint32_t *p holding address 0x1000, what address does p + 3 hold?","o":["0x100C: pointer arithmetic moves by 3 × sizeof(uint32_t) = 12 bytes","0x1003","0x1018","It depends on the compiler"],"a":0,"w":"Adding n to a pointer advances it by n elements, each 4 bytes here: 0x1000 + 12 = 0x100C."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Structs, Arrays & Strings in C","f":"p2-structs-arrays-strings.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Inside void f(char buf[64]) , what is sizeof buf ?","o":["64","65","1","8: an array parameter is really a pointer"],"a":3,"w":"Array parameters decay to pointers; the 64 is ignored. That's why C functions take an explicit length."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Structs, Arrays & Strings in C","f":"p2-structs-arrays-strings.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What made Heartbleed possible?","o":["A weak encryption algorithm","Copying as many bytes as a length field in the request claimed, without checking it against the real request size","A stack overflow in a recursive function","A race condition between two threads"],"a":1,"w":"The heartbeat handler trusted the peer's length field and copied up to 64 KiB of adjacent heap memory into the reply. One bounds check fixed it."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Structs, Arrays & Strings in C","f":"p2-structs-arrays-strings.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How does the kernel's read() system call reach the right code for a pipe versus an ext4 file?","o":["A long switch statement on the file type","Separate system calls for each file type","It calls through the function pointer in the file's file_operations table, set when the file was opened","The C library decides before entering the kernel"],"a":2,"w":"f->f_op->read(...) is an indirect call through a struct of function pointers: the kernel's way of expressing an interface in C."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"How a CPU Runs Your Code","f":"p3-how-cpu-runs-code.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does the x86-64 call instruction do?","o":["Jumps to the function and saves all registers","Pushes the address of the next instruction onto the stack, then sets rip to the function's address","Creates a new thread for the function","Asks the kernel to run the function"],"a":1,"w":"call pushes the return address (rsp −= 8) and jumps. ret pops it back into rip . Saving other registers is the compiled function's job."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"How a CPU Runs Your Code","f":"p3-how-cpu-runs-code.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"On Linux x86-64, where does a function find its first integer argument?","o":["On the stack at rsp","In rax","In rip","In the rdi register"],"a":3,"w":"The System V calling convention passes the first six integer or pointer arguments in rdi, rsi, rdx, rcx, r8 and r9; the return value comes back in rax."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"How a CPU Runs Your Code","f":"p3-how-cpu-runs-code.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What must the kernel save so that a paused thread can later continue exactly where it stopped?","o":["Its registers, including rip and rsp","A copy of its whole stack","Its source code line number","Nothing; threads restart from the beginning"],"a":0,"w":"The stack stays in memory where it is. The register values, especially the program counter and stack pointer, are what define \"where it was\", and a context switch saves and restores them."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"How a CPU Runs Your Code","f":"p3-how-cpu-runs-code.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Roughly what does a mispredicted branch cost on a modern x86 core?","o":["Nothing; prediction is free","About 1 cycle","About 15–20 cycles of discarded pipeline work","About a millisecond"],"a":2,"w":"The pipeline has to be flushed and refilled from the correct path. That's cheap in absolute terms but adds up quickly in a hot loop."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"How a CPU Runs Your Code","f":"p3-how-cpu-runs-code.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How did operating systems mitigate Meltdown?","o":["By disabling the CPU cache","With kernel page-table isolation: unmapping the kernel from user page tables, so syscalls and interrupts switch page tables","By removing system calls","By running all programs in kernel mode"],"a":1,"w":"KPTI keeps kernel memory out of the address space while user code runs, so speculative reads have nothing to read, at the cost of extra work on every kernel entry and exit."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"The Memory Hierarchy","f":"p4-memory-hierarchy.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Roughly how long does a read from main memory (DRAM) take when it misses every cache?","o":["About 1 ns","About 10 µs","About 100 ns","About 10 ms"],"a":2,"w":"Around 80–100 ns: a hundred times an L1 hit, and hundreds of simple instructions' worth of time."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"The Memory Hierarchy","f":"p4-memory-hierarchy.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Your code reads one 4-byte int that is not in any cache. How much data moves from DRAM into the cache?","o":["A whole 64-byte cache line","Exactly 4 bytes","A 4 KiB page","The whole array"],"a":0,"w":"Caches transfer whole lines, 64 bytes on x86-64. The neighbouring 60 bytes come along, which is why spatial locality pays."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"The Memory Hierarchy","f":"p4-memory-hierarchy.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Which access pattern makes the best use of the cache for a large C array int m[R][C] ?","o":["Outer loop over columns, inner loop over rows","Random order, to spread the load","It makes no difference","Outer loop over rows, inner loop over columns"],"a":3,"w":"C is row-major, so m[r][c] and m[r][c+1] are adjacent. Varying the last index fastest walks memory in order."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"The Memory Hierarchy","f":"p4-memory-hierarchy.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"An NVMe SSD streams 5 GB/s, yet one random 4 KiB read takes ~80 µs. How can both be true?","o":["The 5 GB/s figure is false advertising","Throughput comes from many requests in flight at once; latency is the time for one","Only reads from the page cache reach 5 GB/s","The SSD is faster for random reads than sequential ones"],"a":1,"w":"Latency and throughput are different measures. With dozens of overlapping requests, each still taking ~80 µs, the device moves gigabytes per second."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"The Memory Hierarchy","f":"p4-memory-hierarchy.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why does Kafka rely on the OS page cache and sequential disk writes instead of a big in-process cache?","o":["Java cannot allocate large arrays","Disks are faster than RAM","Sequential I/O is fast at every level, and the kernel already caches recent file data in RAM, so a second cache would duplicate it","The page cache is persistent across power loss"],"a":2,"w":"Kafka's design leans on access patterns: append and read logs in order, and let the kernel's page cache hold the hot tail. The page cache is not durable; that's what fsync is for (Chapter 31)."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Bits, Hex & Binary Data","f":"p5-bits-hex-binary.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is 0xff in decimal?","o":["256","255","15","127"],"a":1,"w":"15 × 16 + 15 = 255: eight bits, all set."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Bits, Hex & Binary Data","f":"p5-bits-hex-binary.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How do you turn on O_NONBLOCK in an existing flags value without changing the other flags?","o":["flags = O_NONBLOCK;","flags &= O_NONBLOCK;","flags ^= ~O_NONBLOCK;","flags |= O_NONBLOCK;"],"a":3,"w":"OR sets the bit and leaves every other bit alone. Plain assignment wipes the other flags; AND would clear them."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Bits, Hex & Binary Data","f":"p5-bits-hex-binary.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"With 4 KiB pages, what is the offset within the page of address 0x00403a7c ?","o":["0xa7c","0x403","0x7c","0x3a7c"],"a":0,"w":"4 KiB = 2 12 , so the offset is the low 12 bits: the last three hex digits, 0xa7c . The page number is 0x403 ."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Bits, Hex & Binary Data","f":"p5-bits-hex-binary.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"On an x86 machine, how is the 32-bit value 0x12345678 laid out in memory, lowest address first?","o":["12 34 56 78","21 43 65 87","78 56 34 12","It depends on the compiler"],"a":2,"w":"x86 is little-endian: the least significant byte, 0x78 , comes first. Network byte order is the reverse, which is why htonl exists."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Bits, Hex & Binary Data","f":"p5-bits-hex-binary.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why did the Boeing 787's generator controllers need a reboot at least every 248 days?","o":["Their memory leaked","A signed 32-bit counter of hundredths of a second would overflow after about 2 31 ÷ 100 seconds","Their clocks drifted","The disk filled up with logs"],"a":1,"w":"2,147,483,648 hundredths of a second is about 248.6 days. Integer width is a design decision with an expiry date."}];
